# Bateria. Arch: sysfs (BAT* to symlinki, więc glob, a nie find -type d).
# FreeBSD: sysctl hw.acpi.battery.*, acpi_battery(4); state: 2 = ładuje, 7 = brak.
# Ustawia reply=(kolor ikona procent); bez baterii zwraca 1.
battery_info() {
    local cap bat_status bat

    if [[ $OSTYPE == freebsd* ]]; then
        cap=$(sysctl -n hw.acpi.battery.life 2>/dev/null)
        local -i state=$(sysctl -n hw.acpi.battery.state 2>/dev/null)
        (( state == 7 )) && return 1
        (( state & 2 )) && bat_status=Charging
    else
        for bat in /sys/class/power_supply/BAT*(N); do
            [[ -r $bat/capacity ]] || continue
            cap=$(<$bat/capacity)
            [[ -r $bat/status ]] && bat_status=$(<$bat/status)
            break
        done
    fi
    [[ $cap == <0-100> ]] || return 1

    local icon="󰁹"

    [[ "$bat_status" == "Charging" ]] && icon="󰂄"

    if (( cap < 20 )); then
        reply=(red $icon $cap)
    elif (( cap < 50 )); then
        reply=(yellow $icon $cap)
    else
        reply=(green $icon $cap)
    fi
}

# To customize prompt, run `p10k configure` or edit ~/.p10k.zsh.
[[ ! -f ~/.p10k.zsh ]] || source ~/.p10k.zsh

# p10k składa RPROMPT od nowa przy każdym prompcie, więc RPROMPT='$(battery_info)'
# nigdy się nie pokazywał. Z p10k bateria jest segmentem dopisanym tutaj
# (~/.p10k.zsh zostaje bez zmian), bez p10k zostaje RPROMPT.
function prompt_battery_info() {
    battery_info && p10k segment -f $reply[1] -t "$reply[2] $reply[3]%%"
}
if (( $+functions[p10k] && $+POWERLEVEL9K_RIGHT_PROMPT_ELEMENTS )); then
    (( $POWERLEVEL9K_RIGHT_PROMPT_ELEMENTS[(Ie)battery_info] )) ||
        POWERLEVEL9K_RIGHT_PROMPT_ELEMENTS+=(battery_info)
    p10k reload
elif (( ! $+functions[p10k] )); then
    setopt prompt_subst
    RPROMPT='$(battery_info && print -rn -- "%F{$reply[1]}$reply[2] $reply[3]%%%f")'
fi
